;; stdlib-linearity-affinity-plan (L1): Promise is a :linear resource handle.
;;
;; A Promise is the producer end of a one-shot cell and must be consumed exactly
;; once. promise-fulfill / promise-fail / promise-free all consume the Promise
;; by value. Under -Xlinear this means settling (or freeing) the same Promise
;; twice is a TUR-E0101 -- the double-fulfill bug class is caught at compile
;; time (see errors/promise-linear-double-fulfill) -- and dropping a Promise
;; without ever consuming it is a TUR-E0100.
;;
;; Here promise-free is the single, leak-clean consumption: it releases the
;; shared FutureCell. (A Promise that is fulfilled instead hands ownership of
;; the cell to whoever holds the Future read-end; see
;; docs/archive/history/stdlib-future-linearity-aliasing.md for why the checker cannot
;; yet track that producer/consumer alias, which is why Future stays non-linear.)
;;
;; Expected output: promise ok
(load "stdlib/future.tur")

(defn main [] : int
  (let [p (promise-new)]
    ;; the single consumption -- releases the cell
    (promise-free p))
  (println "promise ok")
  0)
